_model: proposition
---
title: Proposition 1
---
order: 1
---
short_label: VI.1
---
sections:

#### prop_section ####
kind: proposition
----
statement: Triangles and parallelograms which are under the same height are to one another as their bases.
----
proof:

Let {ACB} and {ACD} be triangles, and let {CE} and {CF} be parallelograms under the same height.

I say that the base {CB} is to the base {CD} as the triangle {ACB} is to the triangle {ACD}, and as the parallelogram {CE} is to the parallelogram {CF}.

Produce {BD} in both directions to the points {H} and {L}. Make any number of straight lines {BG} and {GH} equal to the base {CB}, and any number of straight lines {DK} and {KL} equal to the base {CD}. Join {AG}, {AH}, {AK}, and {AL}. [!just I.3]

<figure class="diagram">
<canvas id="canvas_0" width="520" height="230" tabindex="0"></canvas>
<noscript><img alt="VI.1 diagram" src="propVI1.gif"/></noscript>
<script type="text/javascript">
    // Joyce's figure draws HL whole, the four joins from A, the two filled
    // triangles and the two filled parallelograms. The proof also names the
    // equal pieces of the base line (CB, BG, GH, CD, DK, KL), the multiples
    // CH and CL, and the triangles ABG, AGH, ACH, ACL: all are added as
    // transparent highlight targets (explicit ;0 faces on the triangles).
    //
    // "Produce BD in both directions": BH and DL are solid slide twins over
    // Joyce's HL, hidden at rest (initiallyHidden), so each produce strokes
    // outward from its own end; HL itself stays out of the slide sets.
    //
    // Highlighting a filled parallelogram would promote its face over the
    // triangle inside it (#140), so the parallelograms the prose calls CE and
